data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","sequence_number":2529,"output_index":1,"item":{"type":"function_call","id":"fc_0aa41dd63d0397f1016adb482d5f28819191576c6dab5c180c","call_id":"call_05c6b8b33e6e4534a68ea510c8999410","name":"submit_draft","arguments":"{\"artifact\":{\"base_input\":\"prior-candidate.json\",\"patches\":[{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/propositions/-\",\"value\":{\"id\":\"M008\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"For every extended metric space X with measurable space and Borel structure, real exponent d and set A, Measure.hausdorffMeasure d A equals the supremum over positive scales r of the infimum over Nat-indexed families U covering A with ediam(U i)≤r of the sum over i of the supremum over proofs U i nonempty of ediam(U i)^d.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"Hausdorff measure\"],\"source\":{\"statement\":\"For every extended metric space X with measurable space and Borel structure, real exponent d and set A, Measure.hausdorffMeasure d A equals the supremum over positive scales r of the infimum over Nat-indexed families U covering A with ediam(U i)≤r of the sum over i of the supremum over proofs U i nonempty of ediam(U i)^d.\",\"hypotheses\":[\"X has an extended metric, a measurable space, and its Borel measurable-space structure.\"],\"declaration\":\"MeasureTheory.Measure.hausdorffMeasure_apply\",\"module\":\"Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Measure.Hausdorff\",\"evidence\":[]}}},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/propositions/-\",\"value\":{\"id\":\"M009\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"For every measure space (α,m,μ) and ENNReal-valued functions f,g, if f≤g pointwise, then ∫⁻ a, f a ∂μ ≤ ∫⁻ a, g a ∂μ.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[],\"source\":{\"statement\":\"For every measure space (α,m,μ) and ENNReal-valued functions f,g, if f≤g pointwise, then ∫⁻ a, f a ∂μ ≤ ∫⁻ a, g a ∂μ.\",\"hypotheses\":[\"f and g are functions from α to ENNReal and f≤g pointwise.\"],\"declaration\":\"MeasureTheory.lintegral_mono\",\"module\":\"Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.Lebesgue.Basic\",\"evidence\":[]}}},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/propositions/-\",\"value\":{\"id\":\"M010\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"For a measure space, measurable set S and c:ENNReal, ∫⁻ a, 1_S(a)c ∂μ = c·μ(S).\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[],\"source\":{\"statement\":\"For a measure space, measurable set S and c:ENNReal, ∫⁻ a, 1_S(a)c ∂μ = c·μ(S).\",\"hypotheses\":[\"S is measurable and c is ENNReal.\"],\"declaration\":\"MeasureTheory.lintegral_indicator_const\",\"module\":\"Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.Lebesgue.Basic\",\"evidence\":[]}}},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/propositions/-\",\"value\":{\"id\":\"M011\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"For real a,b, Lebesgue volume of [a,b] is ENNReal.ofReal(b−a).\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[],\"source\":{\"statement\":\"For real a,b, Lebesgue volume of [a,b] is ENNReal.ofReal(b−a).\",\"hypotheses\":[],\"declaration\":\"Real.volume_Icc\",\"module\":\"Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Measure.Lebesgue.Basic\",\"evidence\":[]}}},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/propositions/-\",\"value\":{\"id\":\"M012\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"For a Borel extended metric space, set A and d:NNReal, if Hausdorff measure at exponent d is not top, then dimH(A)≤↑d.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"dim_H\",\"Hausdorff measure\"],\"source\":{\"statement\":\"For a Borel extended metric space, set A and d:NNReal, if Hausdorff measure at exponent d is not top, then dimH(A)≤↑d.\",\"hypotheses\":[\"The Hausdorff measure at the NNReal exponent d is not top.\"],\"declaration\":\"dimH_le_of_hausdorffMeasure_ne_top\",\"module\":\"Mathlib.Topology.MetricSpace.HausdorffDimension\",\"evidence\":[]}}},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/inputs/-\",\"value\":\"M008\"},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/inputs/-\",\"value\":\"M009\"},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/inputs/-\",\"value\":\"M010\"},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/inputs/-\",\"value\":\"M011\"},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/inputs/-\",\"value\":\"M012\"},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/0/uses/-\",\"value\":\"M012\"},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/1/uses/-\",\"value\":\"M008\"},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/1/uses/-\",\"value\":\"M009\"},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/1/uses/-\",\"value\":\"M010\"},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/1/uses/-\",\"value\":\"M011\"},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/-\",\"value\":{\"input\":\"M008\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"Instantiate with the current ambient extended metric space, set A equal to E or F, and the real exponent corresponding to the positive NNReal exponent; unfold the supplied Nat-indexed scale content.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"The Euclidean ambient space has an extended metric, measurable structure and Borel structure.\"]}},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/-\",\"value\":{\"input\":\"M009\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"Use the pointwise inequality from the slicing-cover argument on [0,1] with restricted Lebesgue measure.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"The integrands are ENNReal-valued functions and the stated cover inequality holds pointwise.\"]}},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/-\",\"value\":{\"input\":\"M010\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"Apply to the measurable set J_i∩[0,1] and constant ρ_i^s.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"J_i∩[0,1] is Borel measurable; ρ_i^s is ENNReal.\"]}},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/-\",\"value\":{\"input\":\"M011\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"Use the closed interval enclosure of J_i∩[0,1] and its endpoints, whose difference is at most ρ_i+η_i.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[]}},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/-\",\"value\":{\"input\":\"M012\",\"at\":\"S001\",\"bindings\":\"Apply to F with NNReal exponent corresponding to q+1 after proving its Hausdorff measure is zero, hence not top.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"F lies in a Borel extended metric space; q+1 is a nonnegative real exponent.\"]}},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/0/text\",\"value\":\"Let D=dim_H(E) and F=E×[0,1] with the stipulated concatenated Euclidean metric. First, 0≤D≤n<∞: for n=0 the ambient space is a singleton. For n>0, compactness gives a bounded box containing E; cover the box by a grid of cubes of side h. For 0<h≤1 the number is at most C h^(−n), for a constant C depending only on the box and n, each cube has Euclidean diameter at most √n h, and for every real m>n their total m-power diameter cost tends to zero. This local covering argument proves D≤n using the Hausdorff dimension threshold characterization. For the upper bound, fix q>D. The source theorem M003 gives H^q(E)=0. Apply M012 to the product cover construction to convert zero Hausdorff measure of F at exponent q+1 into dim_H(F)≤q+1. The scale content used here is the Nat-indexed cover infimum; its identification with Hausdorff measure is obtained by applying M008, with no normalization factor. At n=0, F is isometric to [0,1], and its dimension is 1 by M005. Suppose n>0. The zero-measure conclusion for E gives, by the exact scale formula M008, arbitrarily small q-cost covers at each positive scale. Replace zero-diameter nonempty sets by small open balls with total additional q-cost arbitrarily small. Partition [0,1] into at most 1/r_j+1 intervals of lengths at most r_j for each positive diameter r_j≤1. Products have diameter at most √2 r_j, and total (q+1)-cost is at most 2(√2)^(q+1)Σ r_j^q, tending to zero. Thus H^(q+1)(F)=0; M012 yields dim_H(F)≤q+1. Let q decrease to D to obtain dim_H(F)≤D+1.\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/1/text\",\"value\":\"For D>0 fix 0<s<D. Define C_a(A,δ) as the infimum of the tsum of ediam(U_i)^a over Nat-indexed δ-covers. M008 states exactly that Hausdorff measure is the supremum over positive δ of this content: the nonempty-witness supremum contributes the same term as the ordinary power because ediam(∅)=0 and a>0. Since smaller-scale admissible covers form a subset of larger-scale covers, C_a(A,δ) is monotone as δ decreases; hence its limit at zero equals that supremum. M001 gives H^s(E)=∞, so C_s(E,δ)→∞. Fix any Nat-indexed δ-cover V_i of F, let ρ_i=ediam(V_i), and project to A_i and B_i. Both coordinate projections are 1-Lipschitz for the concatenated metric, so projected diameters are at most ρ_i. Choose η_i=ε·2^(−(i+1))/(1+ρ_i^s), and an open interval J_i containing B_i of length at most ρ_i+η_i. For every t∈[0,1], the A_i with t∈J_i cover E. Thus C_s(E,δ)≤Σ_i 1_(J_i∩[0,1])(t)ρ_i^s pointwise. Apply monotonicity of the ENNReal lintegral (M009), then countable Tonelli (M002). Each summand is measurable; M010 computes its integral as ρ_i^s times the restricted measure of J_i∩[0,1]. Enclose that set in a closed interval of length at most ρ_i+η_i; M011 computes its Lebesgue measure as that length, while restricted mass of [0,1] is one. Consequently C_s(E,δ)≤Σ_iρ_i^(s+1)+ε. Let ε tend to zero and take the infimum over all δ-covers to obtain C_s(E,δ)≤C_(s+1)(F,δ). Since the left side tends to infinity, so does the right side, and M008 yields H^(s+1)(F)=∞. M001's lower-threshold theorem then gives s+1≤dim_H(F). Let s increase to D. Throughout, s is represented by its NNReal coercion and the measure formula uses its corresponding real coercion.\"}]}}","status":"completed"}}

